# Everything in this directory is sent to the Docker daemon before a build can
# start, once per `docker build`. Only a few things actually need to travel:
# the selected Dockerfile under docker/, the Qt source archive, the macOS SDK
# tarball, and the small directories the Dockerfiles COPY (patches/, scripts/,
# tests/). Without this file the working directory went too - which meant the
# scratch downloads in tmp/ (a 1.4 GB SDK zip, at one point), the multi-megabyte
# build logs and the whole .git history were shipped over and thrown away on
# every single build.
#
# The macOS SDKs travel from downloads/ (docker/qt*/macos/*/Dockerfile COPY
# the one their build arguments name).

.git
.gitignore
tmp/
artifacts/
build/
references/
*.log
README.md
CHANGELOG.md
docs/

# cmake/ is not listed: the Qt 6 Dockerfiles COPY the toolchain files out of
# it.

# The same tarballs at the top level, from before downloads/ existed. Nothing
# reads them any more, but a working copy that predates the move still has them,
# and leaving them in the context doubles what is sent for no purpose. The
# leading slash matters: it pins these to the root, so downloads/ is untouched.
/qt-everywhere-*.tar.xz
/MacOSX*.sdk.tar.*

# Operating-system media for the test VMs (Jaguar, Lion). The VM scripts use
# them from the host; no Dockerfile COPYs them, and at several GB they would
# otherwise travel with the context of every build.
downloads/jaguar/
downloads/lion/

# Backup archives at the top: no Dockerfile COPYs them.
/*.zip
